1
00:00:01,000 --> 00:00:05,238
Orchard Core can act as an identity provider for your applications.

2
00:00:05,238 --> 00:00:10,578
One settings page decides which endpoints it offers, which flows applications can use,

3
00:00:10,578 --> 00:00:13,307
and which keys and token options it uses.

4
00:00:13,307 --> 00:00:17,370
In this video, we'll enable the server and go through that page.

5
00:00:21,970 --> 00:00:27,761
With the OpenID Connect Authorization Server feature, client applications can ask your site

6
00:00:27,761 --> 00:00:30,150
to sign users in and issue tokens.

7
00:00:30,150 --> 00:00:34,090
A client can be a website or a service that calls your APIs.

8
00:00:34,840 --> 00:00:38,012
Endpoints are the addresses that applications call.

9
00:00:38,012 --> 00:00:43,299
Flows are the ways an application can get a token, and each flow needs certain endpoints.

10
00:00:43,299 --> 00:00:47,250
Keys are the signing and encryption certificates the server uses.

11
00:00:48,000 --> 00:00:50,797
Applications and scopes have their own pages.

12
00:00:50,797 --> 00:00:54,745
An application can use only the flows that you allow on the server

13
00:00:54,745 --> 00:00:58,200
settings page, so these settings limit every application.

14
00:01:02,500 --> 00:01:05,946
Open Tools, Features, and search for OpenID.

15
00:01:05,946 --> 00:01:12,080
The OpenID Connect features are listed together, under the OpenID Connect category.

16
00:01:12,590 --> 00:01:17,289
Select Enable in the row of OpenID Connect Authorization Server.

17
00:01:17,289 --> 00:01:23,800
Orchard Core also enables the features it depends on, such as OpenID Connect Management UI.

18
00:01:24,282 --> 00:01:29,508
The Settings menu has an OpenID Connect section with Authorization Server.

19
00:01:29,508 --> 00:01:34,342
Applications and Scopes appear under Access Control, OpenID Connect.

20
00:01:38,619 --> 00:01:42,142
Open Settings, OpenID Connect, Authorization Server.

21
00:01:42,142 --> 00:01:46,515
A warning at the top says the website might restart when you save,

22
00:01:46,515 --> 00:01:49,249
which can make it briefly unresponsive.

23
00:01:49,785 --> 00:01:52,981
Until you save, the page shows the defaults.

24
00:01:52,981 --> 00:01:59,435
Every endpoint is enabled, and only the authorization code and refresh token flows are allowed.

25
00:01:59,902 --> 00:02:06,531
Under Endpoints, each Enable checkbox turns on one endpoint, and its address appears beside it.

26
00:02:06,531 --> 00:02:13,752
Enable Token Endpoint is where applications exchange a code, credentials, or a refresh token for tokens.

27
00:02:14,223 --> 00:02:19,108
Enable Authorization Endpoint is where users are sent to sign in and, depending

28
00:02:19,108 --> 00:02:21,866
on the application, to give their consent.

29
00:02:21,866 --> 00:02:26,463
Enable Pushed Authorization Endpoint can be enabled only together with it.

30
00:02:26,943 --> 00:02:31,046
Enable End Session Endpoint lets applications sign users out.

31
00:02:31,046 --> 00:02:34,475
Require logout confirmation asks users to confirm.

32
00:02:34,475 --> 00:02:38,883
When it is cleared, an application that sends a valid hint for the

33
00:02:38,883 --> 00:02:41,393
signed-in user can skip the prompt.

34
00:02:41,937 --> 00:02:47,497
Enable User Info Endpoint answers only for access tokens that represent users.

35
00:02:47,497 --> 00:02:55,307
Enable Introspection Endpoint and Enable Revocation Endpoint add the endpoints for checking and revoking tokens.

36
00:02:59,588 --> 00:03:03,233
Under Flows, you choose how applications can get tokens.

37
00:03:03,233 --> 00:03:07,818
A flow is shown only while the endpoints and flows it needs are enabled.

38
00:03:08,344 --> 00:03:09,578
Let's see that.

39
00:03:09,578 --> 00:03:14,397
When we clear Enable Token Endpoint, every flow that needs it disappears and

40
00:03:14,397 --> 00:03:20,274
is cleared, while Allow Implicit Flow stays, because it only needs the authorization endpoint.

41
00:03:20,735 --> 00:03:23,303
Reloading the page discards that change.

42
00:03:23,303 --> 00:03:27,713
Allow Authorization Code Flow is for users who sign in through a browser:

43
00:03:27,713 --> 00:03:32,235
they sign in on your site, and the application exchanges a code for tokens.

44
00:03:32,709 --> 00:03:37,822
Allow Implicit Flow returns tokens straight from the authorization endpoint.

45
00:03:37,822 --> 00:03:42,499
Allow Hybrid Flow returns an authorization code together with tokens.

46
00:03:42,981 --> 00:03:49,407
Allow Password Flow lets an application send a user's name and password to the token endpoint.

47
00:03:49,407 --> 00:03:54,870
Allow Refresh Token Flow lets an application exchange a refresh token for fresh

48
00:03:54,870 --> 00:03:59,561
tokens, and needs the password, authorization code, or hybrid flow.

49
00:04:00,136 --> 00:04:05,825
Allow Client Credentials Flow is for applications that act as themselves, with no user.

50
00:04:05,825 --> 00:04:11,635
The application authenticates with its own client identifier and secret, and receives the

51
00:04:11,635 --> 00:04:13,286
roles assigned to it.

52
00:04:17,572 --> 00:04:21,115
Require Proof Key for Code Exchange is a global setting.

53
00:04:21,115 --> 00:04:26,371
It applies to every registered application, whether or not the application enables it,

54
00:04:26,371 --> 00:04:29,742
so check that your client libraries support it first.

55
00:04:30,203 --> 00:04:36,474
Require Pushed Authorization Requests works the same way, and needs the pushed authorization endpoint.

56
00:04:36,474 --> 00:04:41,293
Let's select it, clear Enable Pushed Authorization Endpoint, and select Save.

57
00:04:41,775 --> 00:04:44,980
The page returns with an error, and nothing is saved:

58
00:04:44,980 --> 00:04:50,466
the pushed authorization endpoint must be enabled when enforcing pushed authorization requests.

59
00:04:50,466 --> 00:04:53,725
The settings are checked this way every time you save.

60
00:04:58,025 --> 00:05:00,156
Advanced Options are optional.

61
00:05:00,156 --> 00:05:04,419
Authority is the base address of this site as the identity server.

62
00:05:04,419 --> 00:05:08,445
When it is empty, Orchard Core builds it from the site's host.

63
00:05:08,981 --> 00:05:13,074
The server uses a signing certificate and an encryption certificate.

64
00:05:13,074 --> 00:05:17,996
If you select none, Orchard Core creates self-signed certificates for the site, and

65
00:05:17,996 --> 00:05:22,421
creates replacements when the existing ones are within a week of expiring.

66
00:05:22,928 --> 00:05:28,079
To use your own, choose a store location for the encryption or the signing certificate.

67
00:05:28,079 --> 00:05:31,237
Then choose a store name, and then the certificate.

68
00:05:31,237 --> 00:05:34,228
Only certificates with a private key are listed.

69
00:05:34,746 --> 00:05:40,029
Disable Rolling Refresh Tokens lets a refresh token be reused until it expires,

70
00:05:40,029 --> 00:05:43,696
instead of being marked as redeemed after it is used.

71
00:05:44,221 --> 00:05:47,847
Token Format sets the format of access tokens.

72
00:05:47,847 --> 00:05:51,333
ASP.NET Core Data Protection is the default.

73
00:05:51,333 --> 00:05:57,330
JSON Web Token (JWT) produces standard access tokens that are signed and, unless

74
00:05:57,330 --> 00:05:59,631
you turn it off, encrypted.

75
00:06:00,111 --> 00:06:07,880
With JSON Web Token (JWT) selected, Disable Access Token Encryption (only for JWT tokens) becomes available.

76
00:06:07,880 --> 00:06:15,104
Selecting it leaves the access tokens unencrypted, which lets third-party resource servers use them.

77
00:06:15,104 --> 00:06:22,191
Use Reference Access Tokens keeps tokens in the database and returns fixed-size tokens to clients.

78
00:06:26,449 --> 00:06:28,968
Let's save a working configuration.

79
00:06:28,968 --> 00:06:34,251
We select Allow Client Credentials Flow and Require Proof Key for Code Exchange,

80
00:06:34,251 --> 00:06:35,909
and then select Save.

81
00:06:36,366 --> 00:06:40,799
A message confirms the update, and the site reloads its configuration.

82
00:06:40,799 --> 00:06:46,516
Applications are registered on their own page, which offers only the flows you allowed here.

83
00:06:48,516 --> 00:06:51,750
That's the OpenID Connect Authorization Server:

84
00:06:51,750 --> 00:06:55,229
endpoints, flows, and keys, set from a single page.

85
00:06:55,229 --> 00:06:56,816
Thanks for watching.

